// @emit-error ref valtype with no interned shape
// A FRONTIER pin, marked at the honest current state: this program is REJECTED, and the
// DECLARED-alias spelling of the same union (`type U = Box<i32> | Tag`, pinned by
// `union-member-generic-application.vl`) compiles and runs.
//
// The asymmetry is where the two spellings are rewritten. A declared union's members live
// in a `UnionDecl`, and `canonEmitTypeNames` resolves a generic-application member there to
// its instantiated shape. An INLINE union annotation is a single `TypeRef` whose name is the
// pipe-joined member set, canonicalized by `canonEmitName`'s union arm — which is
// deliberately NOT given the same rewrite: the same arm canonicalizes `Box<i32> | null`,
// whose NULLABLE-niche lowering resolves the application through its name today
// (`nullablePartOf` peels straight to `Box<i32>`, and the gae registry has interned it from
// the `TypeRef`), so rewriting there trades a working spelling for a broken one.
//
// Reaching this cell needs the member set to be split and each member resolved through the
// same rule the `UnionDecl` arm uses, without disturbing the nullable niche — measured, not
// assumed: `let b: Box<i32> | null` and `f(b: Box<i32> | null)` both run today.
//
// The PARAM position of the same inline spelling rejects differently ("only i32, i64, f64,
// f32, boolean, struct, union, array, or string parameters are supported") — same cause, a
// different classifier ladder reaching it first.
type Box<T> = { v: T }
type Tag = { t: i32 }

const u: Box<i32> | Tag = { v: 1 }
print(1)
